 utilization.\\n**Actual**: Kernel time regressed from 0.0214 ms to 0.0230 ms (-7.6%), while compute SOL remained the dominant component (64.8%) and memory SOL was still secondary (31.2%). (-7.6%)\\n**Analysis**: The diagnosis (compute-side limitation) is plausible because compute SOL is much higher than memory SOL. However, the specific configuration changes did not improve performance, so the fix was ineffective. Increasing BLOCK_K and BLOCK_N and changing matrix/wave settings likely hurt occupancy, scheduling balance, register pressure, or instruction mix, offsetting any potential MFMA gains. Since runtime worsened despite verification passing, this tuning moved the kernel away from its local optimum rather than unlocking higher arithmetic throughput.\\n**Lessons**:\\n  - A correct high-level bottleneck diagnosis does not guarantee that a specific knob change will help; MFMA-enabling must be validated with instruction-level and occupancy effects.\\n  - Block-size and wave tuning can easily trade off compute intensity against occupancy/register pressure; larger tiles are not automatically faster.\\n  - Use stepwise tuning (one variable at a time) to isolate which config change causes regression.\\n\\n#### Round 1 Reflection [Ineffective]\\n**Diagnosis**: No matrix-core/tensor-core path is being used, limiting math throughput on MI300X.\\n**Fix Applied**: Retune/rewrite the kernel to force MFMA-enabled Triton codegen on CDNA3 (use HIP-targeted matmul configs and verify generated ISA contains MFMA instructions for fp16/bf16).\\n**Expected**: If compute throughput was the true limiter and MFMA was not being used, enabling MFMA/tensor-core-style codegen should have reduced kernel time and increased effective compute utilization.\\n**Actual**: Kernel time regressed from 0.0214 ms to 0.0256 ms (-19.7%), despite targeting MFMA-friendly Triton settings; verification still passed. (-19.7%)\\n**Analysis**: The observed slowdown is opposite of the expected result for a successful compute-path fix. NCU SOL reports Compute 69% vs Memory 34%, which still suggests compute is more stressed than memory, but the change did not improve performance in that domain. This indicates either (a) the original root cause was incomplete/incorrect (not simply 'no MFMA path'), or (b) the retuned launch/tile parameters (BLOCK_K 32, BLOCK_N 64, removal of matrix_instr_nonkdim and waves_per_eu constraints) hurt occupancy, instruction mix, or scheduling enough to outweigh any MFMA benefit. In short, the hypothesis 'force MFMA => faster' was not validated by runtime.\\n**Lessons**:\\n  - Do not assume MFMA enablement alone guarantees speedup; validate full kernel efficiency (occupancy, register pressure, wave scheduling, and tile reuse).\\n  - When changing multiple knobs at once, regressions are hard to attribute; isolate changes (e.g., MFMA enablement first, then tile-size sweeps).\\n  - Use ISA + profiler correlation: confirm MFMA presence and check whether achieved FLOPs, wave occupancy, and stall reasons improved.\\n\\n#### Round 1 Reflection [Ineffective]\\n**Diagnosis**: Non-tensor-core matmul path limits arithmetic throughput on MI300X.\\n**Fix Applied**: Use Triton configs and dtypes that lower to AMD MFMA/tensor instructions (e.g., FP16/BF16-focused matmul kernel path, verify generated ISA includes MFMA) and retune BLOCK_* accordingly.\\n**Expected**: If compute throughput was the main limiter, switching to MFMA/tensor-core-friendly matmul settings should have reduced kernel time and raised effective compute utilization (or at least improved time at similar utilization).\\n**Actual**: Kernel time regressed from 0.0214ms to 0.0245ms (-14.4%), despite targeting the compute path; verification passed but performance worsened. (-14.4%)\\n**Analysis**: The diagnosis appears directionally correct because SOL shows compute (62.4%) higher than memory (32.0%), consistent with a compute-leaning bottleneck. However, the applied changes were too broad and likely 